A||N||7||fieroluke||Low idle on rebuilt 87 Iron Duke||||5||
Z||000000||fieroluke||06-08-2001||09:11 AM||||Hi everybody,<P>we have recently rebuilt an 87 L4 Fiero engine, and the engine runs great, EXCEPT:<P>As the engine warms up, the idle drops gradually. When the engine is at operating temp, the idle is sometimes as low as 700 rpm. Is this intentional (fuel saving maybe)?<P>I verified that the ECM measures rpm correctly, but as soon as I hook up the scanner, idle returns to 1000, and the scanner displays the correct idle, and desired idle as 1000 as well.<P>Now, if I had a mechanical or other problem on the engine, and the ECM couldn't keep the idle +/-50 or so of the desired idle, I would get a trouble code and lamp, right? But that's not the case, so I'm wondering if GM wanted to bring down fuel consumption by lowering the idle in "D".<P>If I switch to "N" or "P", idle goes up immediately as well. I don't mind fuel economy (at $3.80 a gallon, who does?), but the problem with the low idle is, if I start the heat soaked engine, the idle is normal, but as soon as I switch to "D" , die idle goes down, and this kills the engine sometimes, which is embarrassing. <P>Also, the low idle sometimes makes the entire car shake (resonance), which can be annoying.<P>What are your experiences?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaveL||06-08-2001||09:32 AM||||I have an 87 2.5 also, and there's nothing wrong with your idle speeds. In drive, mine runs as low as 600 or so. With the ultimate dogbone in mine, the idle does sometimes cause noticeable vibration, but I just tell any passengers that happen to be with me that V-8s do that! As far as stalling, make sure you have a new injector installed, clean fuel filter, and good plugs & wires. That should stop the majority of it.<BR>Happy Ferrarro'ing !||||reg||10
Z||000002||PBJ||06-08-2001||09:33 AM||||sounds like the throttle body and iac valve and porting needs a good cleaning with a good cleaner and brush.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroluke||06-08-2001||09:44 AM||||Hmm, I also have the ultimate dogbone, so that explains the vibration. Whatever posessed Pontiac to go to the single mount transmission mount is beyond my comprehension, but that's another story.<P>As for the IAC, true, I can give that one a cleaning, but if the idle is what the ECM wants it to be, will that make a difference at all? I'll give it a try anyway.  As for the injector, I think replacing it is a bit expensive, but I think there's a filter on/inside the injector, right? I have not replaced that one.<P>Other than that, the rebuild included new plugs, wires, DIS coils/module/sensor and air temp sensor (which was off by almost 20F). The other sensors seem fine, although I'm tempted to replace the MAP sensor, just in case. Is it interchangeable with the V6 MAP?<P>Oh, and of course it got new fuel, oil and K&N air filters too. I don't remember if the PCV valve was replaced though. Have to check my receipts...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fieroluke (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||fieroluke||06-08-2001||10:10 AM||||<BR>Well, but if 600 is really the desired idle for the automatic, I'll change the idle table in the ECM (once I recover my disassembly from my harddrive, of which the Windows installer thought it would be a great idea to overwrite the partition table :-( ).<BR>600 is just a bit too low for my taste. Even the 87 V6 has an idle of 850, so maybe I'll go for 700 or even 750. I'll see...<P>Someone with an 88 L4 recently told me his idle was around 950 (manual transmission), which is the same as the V6 with a manual.<BR>Why is the L4 idle so much lower than the V6 idle then?<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/th_88gt.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaveL||06-08-2001||03:57 PM||||If you rebuilt your engine without replacing and or cleaning the IAC, fuel filter, etc., you may have missed something. That injector is 14 years old, how many gallons of gasoline have gone through it? Only $60 to 80, very easy to replace.||||reg||10
Z||000006||Leadfoot||06-08-2001||06:40 PM||||I highly doubt this is your problem but it's good info anyway...expectially if you own an L4.  I had an 86 Duke and that SOB (sweet old block) always idled low.  I was doing a head job on it (yeah very funny) and found that the #3 exhaust valve wasn't sitting tight.  I put new valves in it and lapped them (el cheapo special) and the engine r's were up by almost 200. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||06-08-2001||07:52 PM||||REcheck the charging system and the various wires bolted to the block & bell housing. Those are known to cause batty idle problems.||||reg||1
